---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
---
<Base title="Patch" description="A small model of plants you can play with. Change the rain, the heat or the CO2 in six places around the world and watch what grows. A teaching toy that shows how process-based vegetation and crop models work.">
  <article class="pm">
    <section id="sim" class="sim">
      <header class="simtop">
        <div class="ttl"><h1>Patch</h1><p class="tag">A small model of plants. Change the world and see what grows.</p></div>
        <div class="acts">
          <button class="btn quiet tourback" type="button" hidden>Take the tour</button>
          <button class="btn quiet reset" type="button">Back to today</button>
          <button class="btn quiet share" type="button">Share</button>
        </div>
      </header>
      <p class="shared" aria-live="polite" hidden></p>

      <div id="tour" class="tour" role="region" aria-label="Guided tour">
        <div class="tbody">
          <p class="tstep"><b class="tn"></b><span class="tt"></span></p>
          <p class="tnote" aria-live="polite"></p>
        </div>
        <div class="tbtns">
          <button class="btn tdo" type="button">Do it for me</button>
          <button class="btn tnext" type="button" hidden>Next</button>
          <button class="btn tmore" type="button" hidden>Questions to try</button>
          <button class="btn quiet tclose" type="button">Skip the tour</button>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="placebar">
        <div class="chips places" role="group" aria-label="Place"></div>
        <div class="seg" data-land role="group" aria-label="What grows here">
          <button type="button" class="on" data-v="natural">Wild plants</button>
          <button type="button" data-v="crop">A field of grain</button>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="main">
        <div class="panel p1"><label class="phead"><span class="sr-only">First graph</span><select></select></label><div class="pbody"></div><div class="pfoot"><button class="btn go" type="button">Play a year</button><p class="caption" aria-live="polite"></p></div></div>
        <div class="panel p2"><label class="phead"><span class="sr-only">Second graph</span><select></select></label><div class="pbody"></div></div>
        <div class="side">
          <div class="headline">
            <p class="hl"><b></b><span></span></p>
            <p class="hl2"></p>
          </div>
          <div class="grp climate"><h2>Climate</h2><div class="knobs">
            <div class="knob"><div class="kn"><label for="k-dT">Temperature</label><button type="button" class="info" data-open="d-sugar">How it works</button><b data-o="dT"></b></div><div class="track"><input type="range" id="k-dT" data-k="dT" data-base="0" min="0" max="4" step="0.5" value="0" /><i class="dev"></i><i class="today"></i></div></div>
          <div class="knob"><div class="kn"><label for="k-rain">Rain</label><button type="button" class="info" data-open="d-water">How it works</button><b data-o="rain"></b></div><div class="track"><input type="range" id="k-rain" data-k="rain" data-base="0" min="-40" max="40" step="5" value="0" /><i class="dev"></i><i class="today"></i></div></div>
          <div class="knob"><div class="kn"><label for="k-co2">CO₂ in the air</label><button type="button" class="info" data-open="d-co2">How it works</button><b data-o="co2"></b></div><div class="track"><input type="range" id="k-co2" data-k="co2" data-base="420" min="280" max="900" step="10" value="420" /><i class="dev"></i><i class="today"></i></div></div>
          </div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="grp farm croponly" hidden><h2>Farming</h2><div class="knobs">
          <div class="knob"><div class="kn"><label for="k-variety">Variety</label><b data-o="variety"></b></div><div class="track"><input type="range" id="k-variety" data-k="variety" data-base="0" min="-20" max="30" step="5" value="0" /><i class="dev"></i><i class="today"></i></div></div>
          <div class="knob"><div class="kn"><label for="k-sow">Sowing day</label><b data-o="sow"></b></div><div class="track"><input type="range" id="k-sow" data-k="sow" data-base="0" min="-30" max="30" step="5" value="0" /><i class="dev"></i><i class="today"></i></div></div>
        </div></div>
      </div>
      <p class="toy"><strong>A teaching toy.</strong> Patch shows how research models of vegetation and crops work, such as LPJmL. It is far simpler than they are, shares no code with them, and is not used in any study.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="deep">
      <h2>Go deeper</h2>
      <details class="more" id="d-rules"><summary>Look inside: the five rules, with the maths</summary><div>
        <p class="lead">Most models you hear about learn patterns from data. This kind works the other way round. Someone writes down how a plant works, as a few rules, and the computer applies them day after day. These are Patch's five. Each can be tried on its own.</p>
        <div class="rules">
          <details class="rule-d" id="d-light"><summary>Rule 1: leaves catch light</summary><p>Everything a plant builds starts as sunlight landing on a leaf.</p><figure class="play rule" id="r-light">
        <svg class="vis" viewBox="0 0 360 150" role="img" aria-label="Sunbeams passing down through layers of leaves and getting thinner."></svg>
        <div class="chips" aria-label="Situations"></div>
        <p class="ctx"></p>
        <label class="sl"><span>Layers of leaves <b data-o="L"></b></span><input type="range" data-k="L" min="0" max="7" step="0.1" value="1" /></label>
        <div class="eq" aria-live="polite"></div>
        <p class="say"></p>
        <details class="maths"><summary>Show the maths</summary>
          <p class="f">share of light caught = 1 − e<sup>−k × LAI</sup>, with k = 0.5</p>
          <p>LAI, the leaf area index, is the area of leaf above each square metre of ground: the "layers" in the slider. This is Beer's law, first applied to plant canopies by Monsi and Saeki (1953). LPJ uses the same form and the same k.</p>
        </details>
      </figure><p class="small"><a href="#sim">Back to the simulator</a></p></details>
          <details class="rule-d" id="d-sugar"><summary>Rule 2: light becomes sugar, if the temperature suits</summary><p>A leaf turns light and CO₂ into sugar. How well depends on warmth, and on which of two kinds of plant it is.</p><figure class="play rule" id="r-sugar">
        <div class="seg" data-opt="plant" role="group" aria-label="Kind of plant">
          <button type="button" class="on" data-v="c3">A beech tree</button>
          <button type="button" data-v="c4">A savanna grass</button>
        </div>
        <svg class="vis" viewBox="0 0 360 150" role="img" aria-label="Two curves of sugar made against temperature: one for the tree, peaking in mild weather, and one for the grass, peaking in hot weather."></svg>
        <ul class="key"><li>Sugar made in a day, at each temperature:</li><li><span class="k c3"></span>beech tree</li><li><span class="k c4"></span>savanna grass</li></ul>
        <div class="chips" aria-label="Situations"></div>
        <p class="ctx"></p>
        <label class="sl"><span>Temperature <b data-o="T"></b></span><input type="range" data-k="T" min="-5" max="50" step="1" value="16" /></label>
        <label class="sl"><span>Light caught <b data-o="sun"></b></span><input type="range" data-k="sun" min="0" max="12" step="1" value="7" /></label>
        <div class="eq" aria-live="polite"></div>
        <p class="say"></p>
        <details class="maths"><summary>Show the maths</summary>
          <p class="f">sugar = light caught × 0.5 × α × (c<sub>i</sub> − Γ*) ÷ (c<sub>i</sub> + 2Γ*) × f(T)</p>
          <p>α is how much CO₂ a leaf fixes per unit of light (0.08 for the first kind of plant, called C3). c<sub>i</sub> is the CO₂ inside the leaf, about 0.8 of the CO₂ outside. Γ* is the CO₂ level at which the leaf only breaks even. It rises with temperature, which is why this plant wastes more in warm air (Farquhar, von Caemmerer and Berry 1980; Bernacchi and others 2001).</p>
          <p>The second kind of plant, C4, pumps CO₂ into its leaves, so the CO₂ term drops out and α = 0.053 (Collatz, Ribas-Carbo and Berry 1992). The factor 0.5 scales one leaf up to a whole canopy (Haxeltine and Prentice 1996). f(T) switches the plant off outside its temperature limits, which are close to those in LPJ (Sitch and others 2003): −4, 20, 25 and 38 °C for the beech, 6, 20, 45 and 55 °C for the grass. Light is in megajoules and sugar in grams of carbon, per square metre per day.</p>
        </details>
      </figure><p class="small"><a href="#sim">Back to the simulator</a></p></details>
          <details class="rule-d" id="d-water"><summary>Rule 3: thirst is a brake</summary><p>Leaves lose water through the same pores that let CO₂ in. When roots cannot keep up, the plant closes the pores and makes less sugar.</p><figure class="play rule" id="r-water">
        <svg class="vis" viewBox="0 0 360 150" role="img" aria-label="A bucket of soil water, and two bars: the water roots can deliver and the water the air asks for."></svg>
        <div class="chips" aria-label="Situations"></div>
        <p class="ctx"></p>
        <label class="sl"><span>Water in the soil <b data-o="w"></b></span><input type="range" data-k="w" min="0" max="100" step="1" value="60" /></label>
        <label class="sl"><span>Air temperature <b data-o="T"></b></span><input type="range" data-k="T" min="5" max="45" step="1" value="27" /></label>
        <label class="sl"><span>Sky <b data-o="sun"></b></span><input type="range" data-k="sun" min="5" max="100" step="5" value="60" /></label>
        <div class="eq" aria-live="polite"></div>
        <p class="say"></p>
        <details class="maths"><summary>Show the maths</summary>
          <p class="f">brake = min(1, supply ÷ demand)</p>
          <p class="f">supply = 5 mm × how full the soil is around the roots</p>
          <p class="f">demand = 1.32 × Δ ÷ (Δ + γ) × R<sub>n</sub> ÷ λ</p>
          <p>Supply against demand is how LPJ decides whether a plant is short of water (Sitch and others 2003); 5 mm a day is the most its roots can deliver. Demand is the Priestley and Taylor (1972) estimate of how much water warm, sunlit air can take up. R<sub>n</sub> is the energy the surface absorbs, Δ says how fast air's capacity for water grows with warmth, and γ and λ are physical constants. Treating soil as a bucket goes back to Manabe (1969). Patch has two buckets, a top one and a deep one.</p>
        </details>
      </figure><p class="small"><a href="#sim">Back to the simulator</a></p></details>
          <details class="rule-d" id="d-co2"><summary>Rule 4: CO₂ is food</summary><p>Sugar is made from CO₂, so more of it helps a leaf. But not every kind of plant.</p><figure class="play rule" id="r-co2">
        <svg class="vis" viewBox="0 0 360 150" role="img" aria-label="Sugar-making against CO2 in the air: a rising curve for trees and wheat, a flat line for maize and savanna grass."></svg>
        <ul class="key"><li><span class="k c3"></span>trees, wheat, rice</li><li><span class="k c4"></span>maize, millet, savanna grass</li></ul>
        <div class="chips" aria-label="Situations"></div>
        <p class="ctx"></p>
        <label class="sl"><span>CO₂ in the air <b data-o="co2"></b></span><input type="range" data-k="co2" min="180" max="900" step="5" value="425" /></label>
        <div class="eq" aria-live="polite"></div>
        <p class="say"></p>
        <details class="maths"><summary>Show the maths</summary>
          <p class="f">gain = (c<sub>i</sub> − Γ*) ÷ (c<sub>i</sub> + 2Γ*), compared with today</p>
          <p>The same term as in rule 2, shown here at 25 °C. As CO₂ rises, the curve flattens: each extra bit helps less (Farquhar and others 1980). For C4 plants the term is already full (Collatz and others 1992). Open-air experiments that pump extra CO₂ over real fields find the same contrast (Ainsworth and Long 2005).</p>
          <p>Water: with more CO₂ a leaf can keep its pores a little more closed and still get enough. Patch lowers the air's demand by 30% of the change in pore opening, because leaves are only partly in control of how much water a whole field loses (Jarvis and McNaughton 1986). That 30% is our own simple choice.</p>
        </details>
      </figure><p class="small"><a href="#sim">Back to the simulator</a></p></details>
          <details class="rule-d" id="d-spend"><summary>Rule 5: sugar is spent</summary><p>Staying alive burns sugar. Only what is left over becomes new leaves, roots or wood.</p><figure class="play rule" id="r-spend">
        <svg class="vis" viewBox="0 0 360 130" role="img" aria-label="One bar for sugar made today, and below it a bar split into breathing, the cost of building, and growth."></svg>
        <div class="chips" aria-label="Situations"></div>
        <p class="ctx"></p>
        <label class="sl"><span>Sugar made today <b data-o="made"></b></span><input type="range" data-k="made" min="0" max="12" step="0.5" value="6" /></label>
        <label class="sl"><span>Living leaves and roots <b data-o="size"></b></span><input type="range" data-k="size" min="20" max="900" step="10" value="300" /></label>
        <label class="sl"><span>Temperature <b data-o="T"></b></span><input type="range" data-k="T" min="0" max="45" step="1" value="20" /></label>
        <div class="eq" aria-live="polite"></div>
        <p class="say"></p>
        <details class="maths"><summary>Show the maths</summary>
          <p class="f">breathing = living tissue × r × g(T)</p>
          <p class="f">g(T) = exp[308.56 × (1 ÷ 56.02 − 1 ÷ (T + 46.02))]</p>
          <p class="f">growth = 0.75 × (sugar − breathing)</p>
          <p>g(T) is the temperature curve of Lloyd and Taylor (1994), equal to 1 at 10 °C. That breathing scales with the amount of living tissue follows Ryan (1991). A quarter of what remains is burnt in building new tissue, as in LPJ (Sitch and others 2003). The rate r is a number we tuned for each kind of plant.</p>
          <p>New growth is shared out in fixed parts: grass puts 40% into leaves and 60% into roots; trees put 35% into leaves, 25% into roots and 40% into wood. Leaves live half a year, roots one year, wood about forty.</p>
        </details>
      </figure><p class="small"><a href="#sim">Back to the simulator</a></p></details>
        </div>
        <p class="lead">The rules feed back on each other. New leaves catch more light and make more sugar for more leaves. But they also drink more water and cost more to keep alive. That is why the outcome is hard to guess, and why it is worth running.</p>
        <p class="small lead">The rest of the bookkeeping: each day, rain soaks into the top soil and what does not fit trickles deeper. Trees catch light first and grass gets what passes through. Grass roots stay near the surface; tree roots also reach the deep soil (Walter 1971). Plants drop their leaves when their soil is dry or the air is cold. Where the coldest month stays above 15.5 °C the tropical kinds of plant grow, elsewhere the temperate kinds (Sitch and others 2003). A crop counts warm days to decide when to flower and ripen (Bondeau and others 2007). Before you see anything, the model has run for 220 years so that plants and soil settle.</p>
      </div></details>

      <details class="more" id="d-ask"><summary>Questions to try</summary><div>
        <p class="lead">Guess first, then test. A clean test changes one thing and leaves the rest as today.</p>
        <div class="qs">
          <div class="q"><h3>Less rain: who loses more, trees or grass?</h3><button class="btn quiet setq" type="button" data-site="niamey" data-scen='{"rain":0.7}' data-graphs="pic,cover">Set it up</button><details class="hint"><summary>Hint</summary><p>Change only the rain and open "What covers the ground". Then do the same in wet Manaus, where there is rain to spare.</p><p>In African savannas, the most tree cover a place can hold rises with rainfall, up to about 650 mm a year (Sankaran and others 2005).</p></details></div>
          <div class="q"><h3>More CO₂: who gains, trees or grass?</h3><button class="btn quiet setq" type="button" data-site="niamey" data-scen='{"co2":600}' data-graphs="pic,cover">Set it up</button><details class="hint"><summary>Hint</summary><p>Change only CO₂. Then look inside rule 4 to see why the two kinds of plant respond differently.</p><p>Many real savannas are filling up with trees and shrubs, and rising CO₂ is one suspected cause (Bond and Midgley 2012).</p></details></div>
          <div class="q"><h3>Is warming bad for plants everywhere?</h3><button class="btn quiet setq" type="button" data-site="aarhus" data-scen='{"dT":3}' data-graphs="pic,all">Set it up</button><details class="hint"><summary>Hint</summary><p>Warm every place by the same amount and open "All six places side by side".</p><p>Satellites show the cold parts of the world getting greener as they warm (Keenan and Riley 2018). Patch agrees only halfway: Yakutsk gains trees but grows less, because Patch thinks it is too dry there. In dry summers the real Siberian forest drinks water from thawing frozen ground (Sugimoto and others 2002), which Patch leaves out. A model's mistakes show what it is missing.</p></details></div>
          <div class="q"><h3>Can more CO₂ make up for less rain?</h3><button class="btn quiet setq" type="button" data-site="niamey" data-scen='{"rain":0.8}' data-graphs="pic,sugar">Set it up</button><details class="hint"><summary>Hint</summary><p>The rain is down by a fifth. Now raise CO₂ until plant growth is back where it was.</p><p>With more CO₂ in the air a leaf can keep its pores more closed and lose less water (rule 4). In open-air experiments with extra CO₂, leaves did keep their pores more closed (Ainsworth and Long 2005).</p></details></div>
          <div class="q"><h3>Why does heat shrink a harvest, even with enough rain?</h3><button class="btn quiet setq" type="button" data-site="niamey" data-land="crop" data-scen='{"dT":2}' data-graphs="pic,nums">Set it up</button><details class="hint"><summary>Hint</summary><p>Open "The numbers" and look at the days from sowing to harvest. A crop ripens by counting warm days, so heat hurries it and leaves less time to fill the grain. Then keep the warming and try a slower variety.</p><p>Each degree of warming is estimated to cut world maize harvests by about 7% and wheat by 6% (Zhao and others 2017).</p></details></div>
          <div class="q"><h3>When is the best day to sow?</h3><button class="btn quiet setq" type="button" data-site="niamey" data-land="crop" data-scen='{}' data-graphs="pic,water">Set it up</button><details class="hint"><summary>Hint</summary><p>Move the sowing day earlier and later and watch the harvest. Sow too early and the seedlings sit in dry soil, waiting for the rains.</p><p>Research models choose sowing days from the climate in much the same way (Waha and others 2012).</p></details></div>
        </div>
      </div></details>

      <details class="more"><summary>How real models differ, and why anyone trusts them</summary><div>
        <div class="two">
          <div>
            <div class="tablewrap"><table class="cmp">
              <thead><tr><th></th><th>Patch</th><th>LPJmL, a research model</th></tr></thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr><th scope="row">Kinds of wild plant</th><td>4, two at a time</td><td>11</td></tr>
                <tr><th scope="row">Kinds of crop</th><td>2 simple grains</td><td>12, plus managed grass and energy crops</td></tr>
                <tr><th scope="row">Where</th><td>6 places</td><td>all land on Earth, in cells about 50 km across</td></tr>
                <tr><th scope="row">Soil</th><td>2 layers</td><td>5 layers down to 3 m, with heat and frozen ground</td></tr>
                <tr><th scope="row">Run before use</th><td>220 years</td><td>5,000 years</td></tr>
                <tr><th scope="row">Also inside</th><td>nothing else</td><td>fire, rivers, irrigation, dams, permafrost, nitrogen</td></tr>
                <tr><th scope="row">Size</th><td>about 300 lines of code</td><td>more than 100,000</td></tr>
              </tbody>
            </table></div>
            <p class="small">LPJmL figures from Schaphoff and others (2018) and von Bloh and others (2018).</p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <p>A research model is trusted for four reasons, none of which is that it looks convincing.</p>
            <p><strong>1. Good inputs.</strong> A model is only as right as the weather records, soil maps and CO₂ measurements it is fed.</p>
            <p><strong>2. Tuning.</strong> Some numbers in the rules are not known exactly. Modellers adjust them until the model matches measurements. Try it: move the dial until the line runs through the dots. (These dots are invented for the demonstration. Real ones come from towers that measure CO₂ moving between plants and air.)</p>
            <figure class="play" id="p-tune">
        <svg class="fit vis" viewBox="0 0 360 150" role="img" aria-label="Monthly sugar production: dots for measurements, a line for the model."></svg>
        <ul class="key"><li><span class="k obsk"></span>measurements</li><li><span class="k c3"></span>the model</li></ul>
        <label class="sl"><span>The uncertain number <b data-o="a"></b></span><input type="range" min="25" max="75" step="1" value="32" /></label>
        <p class="say" aria-live="polite"></p>
        <div class="bar"><button class="btn quiet test" type="button" hidden>Test it somewhere else</button></div>
      </figure>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="two">
          <div>
            <p><strong>3. Testing on something new.</strong> Matching the data you tuned on proves little. The real test is data the model has never seen. LPJmL is checked against satellites, CO₂ towers, river flows and harvest records (Schaphoff and others 2018, part 2).</p>
            <p>We tested Patch too. It loses 10% to 16% of its grain for each degree of warming. Real crops lose about 6% to 7% (Zhao and others 2017). Right direction, but too gloomy, so its crop rules are too simple. That is what testing is for.</p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <p><strong>4. Many models side by side.</strong> Different teams write the rules differently. Running several models on the same question shows where they agree (Warszawski and others 2014).</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div></details>

      <details class="more"><summary>For teachers and workshop hosts</summary><div class="three">
        <p><strong>Five minutes.</strong> The tour at the top. It ends on a question worth a minute of talk: why did nobody need to tell the model where the forest is?</p>
        <p><strong>Fifteen minutes.</strong> The tour, then each pair picks one of the questions, writes down a guess, and tests it. "Share" gives a link that opens the same experiment, so a pair can send its result to the room.</p>
        <p><strong>Forty-five minutes.</strong> Add the five rules. Give each group one rule to explain to the others, and finish with why anyone trusts such models.</p>
      </div></details>

      <details class="more"><summary>What Patch leaves out</summary><div>
        <p class="lead">Nutrients, fire, grazing animals, frost damage, snow, frozen ground, different soils, trees of different ages, pests and people. It has one soil everywhere and invents day-to-day weather from monthly averages. Around Yakutsk the real forest is larch, a tree Patch does not have. Its response to CO₂ is probably too strong, because real plants run short of nutrients. And in Patch a slower crop variety nearly always wins, because it never has to race the end of the rains or the first frost.</p>
        <p class="lead">Treat its numbers as illustrations of how the mechanisms work, not as predictions for any real place. The code is open: <a href="https://github.com/joshimudit/joshimudit.github.io/tree/main/src/lib/patch">model and data on GitHub</a>.</p>
      </div></details>

      <details class="more"><summary>Sources</summary><div><ul class="src">
        <li>Ainsworth, E. A. and Long, S. P. (2005). What have we learned from 15 years of free-air CO₂ enrichment (FACE)? <em>New Phytologist</em> 165: 351–372.</li>
        <li>Allen, R. G. and others (1998). <em>Crop evapotranspiration</em>. FAO Irrigation and Drainage Paper 56. (Sunshine and day length from latitude.)</li>
